00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
syrian government forces  enter marat al-numan in the south of  idlib province  they're looking for rebel fighters from  hayat terer al-sham  an armed group with former links to  al-qaeda the town is largely deserted  and destroyed after months of  bombardment  its recapture marks a significant  advance in president bashar al-assad's  drive to take back  all of syria the general command of the  army and the armed forces announced  cleansing these villages and towns  and it assures that the army will  continue to carry out its constitutional  national and moral duties in order to  hunt down all remaining  armed terrorist groups marat al-numan  sits on the m5 highway which links the  capital damascus to syria's second city  aleppo  as well as to provincial capitals such  as hammer and idlib  before the war it was a major trade  route and the main access road to the  north  the latest drive by russian-backed  government forces to recapture syria's  last opposition enclave has sparked a  new exodus  on monday tens of thousands of civilians  fled towards turkey's border  turkish president recep tayyip erdogan  has accused the russian government  of breaking a ceasefire agreement  in idlib there is now a movement towards  our borders and we have taken our  measures  since december it's estimated that more  than 350 000 people have been displaced  from  idlib province the increase in attacks  is worsening the humanitarian  crisis in northwest syria  but the assad government seems  determined to retake this final piece of  territory  still under the control of opposition  fighters  victoria gatenby al-jazeera
 

